MODULE Generating_Metro_Related_data
FORMAT Pseudocode.
PURPOSE Build metro topology, enumerate candidate routes, and index OD/path/section incidences.
PAPER Sections 4.5.1-4.5.3, Equations (21)-(24); Section 4.7.

SCHEMA
    station_index: canonical station name to immutable zero-based integer index in 0..N-1.
    line_manager: line identifiers, ordered stops, and directed service links.
    route: a continuous same-line segment with endpoints and ordered stations.
    path: ordered route segments and their complete station/line traversal sequence.
    route_graph: directed single-physical-section arcs with line IDs, expanded by incoming-line state.
    Legacy multi-section Route arcs are expanded before shortest/k-shortest search, then may be merged for display.
    transfer_bias: nonnegative enumeration penalty in declared search-cost units.
    path_number: unique identifier in this generated artifact.
    OD_path_dic: ordered real OD pair to distinct candidate path records.
    section_path_dic: directed section to path records using that section.
    path_section_dic: path identifier to its ordered directed sections.
    SUPPORT: candidate availability is subsequently checked against each service snapshot.

PROCEDURE get_same_lines
    INPUT from_station, to_station.
    OUTPUT ordered distinct common line identifiers.
    READ line memberships of from_station.
    READ line memberships of to_station.
    INTERSECT memberships by canonical line identifier.
    REMOVE duplicates.
    ORDER common lines by the declared line registry.
    RETURN the common line list, possibly empty.
    An operating segment requires shared line membership and continuous service.
END PROCEDURE

PROCEDURE dijkstra
    INPUT v_matrix, start_index, end_index, bias.
    INPUT initial_incoming_line for a spur search, or START for a new OD search.
    OUTPUT shortest declared search cost and reconstructed path, or UNREACHABLE.
    REQUIRE valid endpoint indices and nonnegative search costs.
    REQUIRE each movement arc traverses exactly one physical directed section on one line.
    EXPAND multi-section shortcuts before search; preserve their complete station/line sequences.
    EXCLUDE zero-length same-station movement arcs; actual line changes are charged on the next movement.
    INTERPRET absent arcs as unavailable.
    IF start_index equals end_index:
        RETURN zero cost and an explicit zero-length path status.
    END IF
    DEFINE each search state as (station_index, incoming_line_or_START).
    RETAIN incoming-line identity even when arcs represent complete same-line route segments.
    Distinct arrivals at the same physical station can have different remaining transfer costs.
    INITIALIZE the start state with initial_incoming_line, preserving a spur root's line context.
    INITIALIZE distance of every state to infinity.
    INITIALIZE predecessor of every state to absent.
    INITIALIZE visited flags to false.
    SET start-state distance to zero.
    INSERT start state into a minimum-priority frontier.
    WHILE frontier is not empty:
        REMOVE the state u with smallest tentative distance.
        IF u has already been finalized:
            CONTINUE.
        END IF
        MARK u finalized.
        IF u satisfies the target-state condition:
            STOP the search with the current best target.
        END IF
        FOR EACH available outgoing route arc from state u:
            CHECK arc continuity and valid stop count.
            v <- (arc.destination_station, arc.line_identifier).
            arc_cost <- declared station traversal cost.
            IF u.incoming_line is neither START nor arc.line_identifier:
                ADD bias to arc_cost for that actual line change.
            END IF
            candidate_cost <- distance[u] + arc_cost.
            IF candidate_cost improves distance[v]:
                STORE candidate_cost.
                STORE predecessor state and the selected route arc.
                INSERT updated v with a deterministic unique queue tie counter.
            END IF
        END FOR
    END WHILE
    IF no target state was reached:
        RETURN UNREACHABLE with no path object.
    END IF
    SELECT the cheapest finalized arrival-line state at the terminal station.
    RECONSTRUCT route segments by following state-specific predecessor links backward.
    REVERSE the reconstructed sequence.
    VERIFY start, end, and continuity of all segments.
    RETURN complete path cost and an independent path record.
END PROCEDURE

PROCEDURE yen_ksp
    INPUT start_station, terminal_station, k, v_matrix, bias.
    INPUT station_index, line_manager, station_manager.
    OUTPUT at most k distinct feasible loopless candidate paths.
    REQUIRE k is a declared positive integer.
    LOOK UP both station indices.
    KEEP an immutable copy of the original route graph.
    CALL dijkstra for the first shortest path.
    IF no path exists:
        RETURN an empty candidate set with disconnected-OD status.
    END IF
    INITIALIZE accepted_paths with the first path.
    INITIALIZE accepted_signatures with its declared path identity.
    INITIALIZE a minimum-priority candidate collection.
    INITIALIZE candidate_signatures for deduplication.
    WHILE accepted_paths contains fewer than k paths:
        latest <- most recently accepted path.
        ENUMERATE each eligible spur position before its terminal station.
        FOR EACH spur position:
            root <- prefix of latest ending at the spur state.
            COPY the original route graph into a working graph.
            FOR EACH accepted path sharing that exact root:
                IDENTIFY its next directed transition after the root.
                TEMPORARILY exclude that transition in the working graph.
                Use the expanded directed section/line transition identity; no shortcut may bypass exclusion.
            END FOR
            TEMPORARILY exclude every incoming-line state of each physical root-prefix station before the spur.
            ENUMERATE spur positions at every physical station, including former shortcut interiors.
            CALL dijkstra from the spur station to terminal on the working graph.
            SUPPLY the root's incoming line as the spur search's initial line context.
            IF no spur path is feasible:
                DISCARD this spur attempt.
                CONTINUE to the next spur position.
            END IF
            COMBINE root and spur without duplicating their shared boundary station.
            VERIFY all route segments remain continuous.
            VERIFY the resulting path starts and ends at the requested stations.
            CHECK the declared loopless condition.
            RECOMPUTE the total search cost of the complete combined path.
            RANK the complete root-plus-spur cost.
            signature <- ordered station-and-line identity under the declared convention.
            IF signature is neither accepted nor already queued:
                INSERT path with total cost and deterministic tie key.
                RECORD its candidate signature.
            END IF
            DISCARD the working graph after the candidate search.
        END FOR
        IF no valid candidate remains:
            BREAK with fewer than k paths.
        END IF
        REMOVE the lowest-cost candidate.
        IF duplicate or invalid:
            CONTINUE removing candidates until valid or the collection is empty.
        END IF
        IF the collection exhausted without a valid candidate:
            BREAK.
        END IF
        APPEND the valid candidate to accepted_paths.
        RECORD its signature.
    END WHILE
    RETURN accepted_paths with search settings and candidate-count diagnostics.
END PROCEDURE

PROCEDURE yen_ksp.contains_subsequence
    INPUT sequence, subsequence.
    OUTPUT whether subsequence occurs contiguously in sequence.
    IF subsequence has length zero:
        RETURN true under the explicitly declared empty-sequence convention.
    END IF
    IF subsequence is longer than sequence:
        RETURN false.
    END IF
    FOR start position from first through last possible alignment:
        COMPARE every aligned element in order.
        IF all elements match:
            RETURN true.
        END IF
    END FOR
    RETURN false.
    NOTE Yen root matching needs an equal prefix, not merely any interior subsequence.
END PROCEDURE

PROCEDURE find_routes
    INPUT exist_path_number, OD_path_dic, section_path_dic, path_section_dic.
    INPUT start_station, terminal_station, v_matrix, bias.
    INPUT station_index, line_manager, station_manager, k.
    OUTPUT next unused path number and updated three incidence dictionaries.
    top_k_paths <- yen_ksp with the supplied ordered OD pair.
    INITIALIZE paths_info for this OD pair.
    FOR EACH distinct returned path:
        VERIFY endpoints match the OD dictionary key.
        CONSTRUCT path_info.path_number from exist_path_number.
        COPY from_stop and to_stop names.
        COPY start_index and end_index.
        COPY ordered route segments.
        COPY complete station_visit_sequence_index.
        COPY corresponding stable station identifiers.
        DERIVE number_of_stations using the declared endpoint-counting convention.
        DERIVE number_of_transfers from actual line changes.

        RECORD the line sequence and path signature.
        APPEND path_info to paths_info.
        INITIALIZE path_section_dic[path_number] as an empty ordered list.
        FOR EACH route segment:
            CALL print_route_info for the optional route diagnostic.
            FOR EACH consecutive station pair eta, mu on the segment:
                VERIFY it is a physical directed section of the indicated line.
                APPEND that directed section to path_section_dic[path_number].
                ADD the path reference to section_path_dic[(eta,mu)].
            END FOR
        END FOR
        RECONCILE repeated transfer-boundary stations between segments.
        DEDUPLICATE section-to-path references under binary incidence semantics.
        CHECK every path section points back to this path in the inverse dictionary.
        INCREMENT exist_path_number.
    END FOR
    STORE paths_info under OD_path_dic[(start_station,terminal_station)].
    IF no candidate was found:
        STORE explicit empty/disconnected status.
    END IF
    RETURN updated counter and dictionaries.
END PROCEDURE

PROCEDURE print_route_info
    INPUT each_route, line_manager.
    OUTPUT optional human-readable route summary.
    REPORT line identifier, route start, route end, and traversal count.
    LOOK UP the line's canonical ordered station list.
    REPORT stations in the actual traversal direction.
    CHECK that printed endpoints equal the route endpoints.
    IF the route cannot be resolved in the line registry:
        REPORT an invalid-route diagnostic.
    END IF
END PROCEDURE

PROCEDURE Generating_Metro_Related_data
    INPUT excel_path, graph_sz_conn_root, station_index_root, station_manager_dict_root.
    INPUT result_API_root, suzhou_sub_data_file_path, timetable artifact paths.
    INPUT time_interval, OD/path artifact paths, train_filename, date bounds.
    INPUT network_schema and optional already verified network_bundle.
    OUTPUT station topology and the OD/path/section dictionary bundle.
    IF a verified network_bundle is supplied:
        REUSE its station_manager,line_manager and matching immutable manifest without loading again.
    ELSE:
        REQUEST the five declared network paths through MetroRequester_SuZhou,
            explicitly passing network_schema; RECEIVE station_manager and line_manager.
    END IF
    FREEZE station_order and station_index.
    VALIDATE physical adjacency and line-specific directed continuity.
    OBTAIN requested k and search bias from configuration or the interactive helper.
    RECORD their provenance as enumeration settings.
    INITIALIZE a route graph for the declared station order.
    FOR EACH ordered station pair i,j:
        IF i=j, record the diagonal policy and continue without constructing a movement arc.
        common_lines <- get_same_lines(station_i, station_j).
        BUILD valid same-line route alternatives in each allowed direction.
        PRESERVE parallel line alternatives when they differ operationally.
        EXPAND every valid route alternative into its adjacent directed section/line arcs.
        DEDUPLICATE identical directed physical-section/line arcs, retaining parallel distinct line IDs.
        IF no route exists:
            STORE an unavailable arc.
        END IF
    END FOR
    SELECT manual or automatic enumeration mode.
    INITIALIZE path counter and all three dictionaries once per run.
    IF manual mode:
        CALL get_valid_station_input for origin.
        CALL get_valid_station_input for destination.
        APPLY the declared same-station OD policy.
        CALL find_routes for this ordered pair.
        RETURN the resulting candidate and incidence bundle.
    ELSE IF automatic mode:
        FOR EACH origin in station_order:
            FOR EACH destination in station_order:
                IF origin equals destination:
                    RECORD the declared same-station status.
                    CONTINUE.
                END IF
                COPY or immutably reference the unmodified route graph.
                CALL find_routes INSIDE these nested loops.
                KEEP the returned monotonically increasing path counter.
            END FOR
        END FOR
        VERIFY every requested ordered OD pair has a result status.
        BUILD bundle.OD_path_dic.
        BUILD bundle.section_path_dic.
        BUILD bundle.path_section_dic.
        ADD station order, line order, enumeration policy, and input fingerprints.
        VALIDATE reciprocal incidence consistency.
        SAVE the bundle under train_filename or the declared neutral topology filename.
        NOTE train_filename is a legacy name; topology is not demand-fitted training data.
        RETURN the bundle and generation report.
    ELSE:
        REPORT invalid mode and request or require a valid configuration.
    END IF
    Retained timetable/path-probability path arguments belong to downstream conversion stages.
END PROCEDURE

PROCEDURE Generating_Metro_Related_data.get_input_or_default
    INPUT prompt, declared default.
    OUTPUT validated integer option or cancellation status.
    READ the configured value first in noninteractive execution.
    OTHERWISE display the prompt and the explicit default.
    IF input is empty:
        RETURN the declared default with a default-used flag.
    END IF
    PARSE an integer without truncating decimal text.
    IF parsing fails or value violates the option's range:
        REPORT the valid domain and retry or return configuration failure.
    END IF
    RETURN the validated option.
END PROCEDURE

PROCEDURE Generating_Metro_Related_data.get_valid_station_input
    INPUT prompt, station_index.
    OUTPUT canonical station name or cancellation status.
    READ a supplied or interactive station name.
    NORMALIZE only reviewed aliases.
    IF the resulting name is in station_index:
        RETURN that canonical name.
    END IF
    REPORT the unknown name and require a valid station.
END PROCEDURE
